@* Help → Work with an AI agent. Mirrors docs/guides/for-ai-agents.md (ADR 0021). *@
<p>An AI agent such as Claude Code or Claude Desktop can import a service, set up its sign-in and write an endpoint as a <strong>draft</strong>. You review it, test it with <strong>Try it</strong> and publish it.</p>
<ol>
    <li>Get a token with the <strong>author</strong> role and the <code>aggregator.mcp</code> scope. An administrator grants that scope; on a developer machine: <code>http://localhost:5300/dev/token?sub=you&amp;scope=aggregator.author%20aggregator.mcp</code>.</li>
    <li>Point the agent at <code>http://localhost:5300/mcp</code> (plain http, <code>localhost</code>) with the header <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>. Claude Code: <code>claude mcp add --transport http api-aggregator http://localhost:5300/mcp --header "Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;"</code>. Claude Desktop: its config file with the <code>mcp-remote</code> bridge (see the full guide). Its <em>Add custom connector</em> dialog can't reach your machine.</li>
    <li>Ask it for the endpoint you need. When it says the draft is ready, open it under <strong>Endpoints</strong>: it shows "Draft … by you via MCP (…)".</li>
    <li>Read the draft, use <strong>Try it</strong> with a real record, and publish it if it's right.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>What the agent can't do:</strong></p>
<ul>
    <li>publish, roll back or delete;</li>
    <li>count its own try as your test;</li>
    <li>see values from real services: it gets field names and types only, because answers can hold personal or health data;</li>
    <li>read or set passwords and keys: it asks you to set them under <strong>Services</strong>;</li>
    <li>fetch a spec from any address;</li>
    <li>change browser access.</li>
</ul>
<p>Everything it changes is in the activity log with your name and its name.</p>
